# The post-death coherence case, run by the driver AFTER the killed worker has
# been replaced and drained in. It proves the replacement is a WORKING worker:
# it serves a correct 200, leaks no descriptor within the case, and logs nothing
# on the error path around the request.
#
# The pid oracle is STRICT (no pid_may_change) and that is correct: the kill and
# the respawn both happened OUTSIDE this prober invocation, so both of the case's
# probe snapshots see the one settled replacement worker. pid_may_change would be
# a vacuous no-op here; the death-spanning assertions this scenario exists to
# make are the driver's, which compare probe snapshots taken on either side of
# the kill. This case only certifies that whatever worker remains works.
#
# Note it does NOT assert on the error log's KILL line: the log legitimately
# carries "worker process NNNN exited on signal 9" from the driver's kill, which
# is the whole point of the scenario. no_error_log here is scoped to \[error\]
# on THIS request's path -- the signal-9 notice is an nginx-level info/alert
# line, not a per-request error, and the driver's oracle 2 is what asserts it
# positively.

name    the replacement worker serves cleanly
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
delta   fds == 0
no_error_log  \[error\]
